WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.101
We say, you know what?

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.635
What if Alex pitches

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.804
and Austin walks around
with like a printed out

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.473
Excel document basically

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:08.875
and basically stares
people in the eyes

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.577
until they give you
your email.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:11.611
And that's what we did.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.246
So I would spend twenty —
that’s like door

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:15.849
to door sales though
for a newsletter list.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:16.282
I like it.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.151
I would walk around

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.387
the big lecture

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:21.721
hall, 500 people,

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:22.789
and I’d
kind of just stand there.

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:23.523
Give me your email.

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.391
Give me your email.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:25.058
And we’d get, seriously.

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:26.559
Did you get big on them,
like did you puff

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:28.428
your chest up
and hand it to them

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:29.963
be like give me your email?

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:32.432
If I at the time,
if I spoke in front

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.001
of 500 people,
I was getting 500 emails.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:36.369
Because I, you know, there's
the whole

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.038
classic thing, like,
you know,

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.173
the number two fear in life
is a public speaking.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.242
And number one is, is
or number one is public

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:44.477
speaking, two is death
like people rather be in the

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:45.478
in the casket than speaking

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:46.713
at the eulogy.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:47.881
That was me at the time.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.716
And so I was like, well,
I'm going to do this,

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.452
I need those emails.

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.319
What's up, everyone?

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.621
I'm Alex Lieberman.

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.456
And I'm Sophia Amoruso.

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.825
You know,
this is Jesse Pujji

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:02.962
and this is The Crazy Ones.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:03.930
What's up, everyone?

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.665
This is Alex
Lieberman, co-founder

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.067
and chairman
of Morning Brew.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.268
And we were back with

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.270
another episode of The Crazy
Ones,

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.638
the best startup show

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:13.006
on planet Earth.

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:13.673
We're trying

00:01:13.673 --> 00:01:15.041
something a little bit
different today.

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.376
My co-host, Jesse

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.078
and Sofia are on a

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.679
well-deserved break

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.181
at the end of the year.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.215
And so if you're listening

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.516
to or watching this episode,

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.051
I believe it is January 3rd.

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.053
So wishing you
a happy New Year

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:29.122
and a ton of good things
to come.

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:30.423
So here's
what we're trying today.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.492
My co-founder

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.360
Austin and I have talked
about the morning

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.362
Bruce story at length

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.797
on other podcasts,

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:39.833
but very rarely,
I don't even know

00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:41.167
if ever have we talked

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.936
about our story together

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.904
in a moderated conversation.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.473
And so that's what
we're going to be doing.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.707
We're going to be talking
about

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:48.842
the journey of morning brew,

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.310
the idea, the challenges,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.412
the wins,
what comes after the brew,

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:53.680
what the business is
like now,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.914
the future of media.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.317
And to have us have
this conversation

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.452
is our good friend
Sahil Bloom.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.020
Sahil is an investor.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.355
He's a writer, He's

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.324
a prolific tweeter.

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:05.625
He has done
all of the things

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.560
and so we're so lucky
to be joined by him today.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.195
So I'm going to pass the mic
to you.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:09.696
Awesome.

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:11.030
Well, thank you guys
so much for having me.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.232
I'm excited
to get to do this.

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:13.833
I'm going to set one ground

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:16.336
rule at the outset of this,
which is no PR

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.537
bullshit responses

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.805
to anything that goes on.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.440
So that's
the only ground rule.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.275
And other than that, I think
we can kick it right off.

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.043
But excited,
too excited to be here.

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:25.645
You know, I want

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:26.880
to start with something

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.214
that you guys both tweeted

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.415
just recently the other day,

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:30.850
which was a screenshot,
I think.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.886
Austin, you tweeted
at first it was a screenshot

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:34.354
of an email

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:35.622
with names blacked out.

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:36.856
So I'm not going to ask you

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:38.291
who who actually said it.

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:40.426
It'll be in the show notes
and we'll share it.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.395
But basically
it was a rejection email

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.931
from presumably an investor

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.966
criticizing a few factors

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.201
about the morning

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:49.869
brew, in particular

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:51.738
criticizing whether or not
it was ever going to be able

00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:53.373
to make any money,
any revenue.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.308
And what you tweeted
was basically like, look,

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:57.043
if we had listened
to these people

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:58.611
that were doubters
of the idea,

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.313
we wouldn't be where
we're sitting today.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.948
So I want to start there.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.183
What's going
through your head

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.384
when you guys are receiving

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:05.852
presumably a bunch of emails

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:07.353
like this back in 2015?

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:09.088
Yeah, I can start.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:11.524
So in 2015 it

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:12.825
morning

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.027
Brew wasn't
a business, right?

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:15.862
It was a a side project,

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.096
a hobby,

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:18.031
a fun thing

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:20.266
just to stay up to
in the business world

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.168
for ourselves
and help our friends.

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:24.737
And one thing I remember
in the early days

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:27.140
I was actually pretty
surprised about,

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.575
and now I look back

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:30.710
and I think it's awesome
was how relentless Alex

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.345
was about getting in touch

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:33.613
with everyone, right?

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.515
Talking to people,
meeting people,

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.784
whether it was advertisers,
investors, whatever.

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:38.685
And Alex,

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:40.587
it's a professor
in Michigan in the email

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.488
and Alex
emailed the professor

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.356
and most people

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:44.791
had pretty good answers.

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:45.992
Most people

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.027
and we sat down
with most people in Michigan

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.129
in the controversial clubs
and whatever,

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.598
and we sent this email

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:52.699
and we had heard

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:55.168
that this guy,
this professor was a little

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:58.371
abrasive and

00:03:58.371 --> 00:03:59.505
we definitely didn't expect

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.007
to get that email.

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:02.442
I don't blame him,
by the way.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:03.810
Like I think at the time

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.311
we also didn't
think it was a business,

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.813
or at least not a potential
big business.

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.182
So I don't blame him. But

00:04:10.183 --> 00:04:12.085
that was only fuel
to the fire.

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:13.286
Do you remember it
being fuel

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:14.487
at the time though,
or was it

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.322
just like a blow?

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.057
Yeah, I mean, it's easy

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.292
to call it
fuel in hindsight.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.727
You know, like, these type
of things are easy to say.

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:21.995
Like, oh, yeah,
I knew I was going

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:22.795
to keep grinding, whatever.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:25.098
Like Alexis Ohanian
has his with being called

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.466
a rounding error
by the Yahoo!

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:27.066
Team.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:28.534
This is kind of
your moment of that.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:29.469
Was it fuel at the time?

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:31.771
It's so hard to know
how much is actually

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:33.106
like revisionist history.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:34.807
Like even when we tell

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:36.442
the original story
of Morning Brew

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:37.877
and how it started,

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.379
like the original insight,

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:40.780
I oftentimes will ask myself

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.049
the question, is that
the original story?

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:43.283
Right?

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:45.551
Because we've told it
so many times now

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:48.254
that I forgot
there's there's some

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.390
kind of principle
where it's like

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:51.324
if you tell a false

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:52.692
truth enough times

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:53.993
that becomes the truth.

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:56.796
And so, like,
I can remember actually what

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:58.464
the thought was in late

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:00.600
2014 when I started

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.236
writing this thing
and when Austin joined on.

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:06.873
And so I would say for me,

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.708
actually

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:09.342
there was probably

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.077
some fuel from people
saying no.

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:12.211
And by
the way, this teacher,

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:13.913
we're

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:15.448
definitely not
just picking on this teacher

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:18.151
because actually Alexis
Ohanian, he had replied

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.620
to Austin tweet
and basically was like,

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.454
I really like

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:22.922
constructive rejections,

00:05:22.922 --> 00:05:24.724
which I totally agree
with, like this.

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:28.261
This teacher was not a dick

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:29.862
in the tweet
like he was constructive.

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.964
He gave reasons for why

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:33.566
he wouldn't invest for me.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.034
Actually,
when we were fundraising,

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:37.804
the worst thing was like
family

00:05:37.804 --> 00:05:40.306
and friends
or people peripheral

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:41.808
that would just string

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:43.242
along these conversations

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:45.745
and be like
three months of them saying,

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:47.947
Hey, I'm going to look
at the materials tomorrow.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.082
Hey, I have this question.

00:05:49.082 --> 00:05:50.383
They would have you answer
all these questions

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.118
and then after three months,
tell you that

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:53.319
they didn't
want to invest like

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.654
that was the worst.

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:56.189
But yeah,
it was this teacher

00:05:56.189 --> 00:05:58.891
I remember, Mark Cuban,
We also asked him to invest,

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:02.395
and I give so much
props to Mark

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:05.098
because he is someone
that, irrespective of

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:07.800
his success, he still emails
with founders

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.902
at the earliest stages
all the time.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.205
But basically
he had a similar response

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.573
to this teacher
where he was like,

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:14.941
There are a ton
of newsletters.

00:06:14.941 --> 00:06:16.109
I don't get the difference.

00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:17.310
What's the differentiator?

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.811
That was
the gist of what he said.

00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:20.613
I think that was the

00:06:20.613 --> 00:06:21.948
fuel for me actually.

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.950
I think a fair

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.853
bit of the fuel was

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:30.390
people who I think

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:31.357
doubted me

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.760
or belittled me throughout
like middle school

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.762
and high school,
and I felt a chip

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:37.663
on my shoulder
from that for sure.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:40.166
And then I think a piece of
it also was just like,

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:42.301
you know,
you guys know this, But

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.236
I lost my dad

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:44.771
actually
right before the brew

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:45.805
started

00:06:46.172 --> 00:06:46.639
a week

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:47.940
before
junior year of college.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.275
The brew started

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:51.277
first semester
senior year at Michigan.

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.345
And so there was

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:53.679
I would say
that was actually the number

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.515
one driver was this

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:57.784
kind of just focus to

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:00.453
create something of value.

00:07:00.453 --> 00:07:02.255
So kind of the Lieberman
household

00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:04.290
wouldn't have to worry again
because that was actually

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.325
probably
the number one anxiety

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.326
that was taking up

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:08.561
space in my brain.

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:10.263
I don't think I knew that.

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:11.297
The timing

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:12.965
connection
between between your father

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.901
passing away and
the starting of the brew,

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:16.736
which is a
really interesting insight.

00:07:18.037 --> 00:07:18.938
Were you like

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:20.173
an entrepreneurial kid,

00:07:20.173 --> 00:07:22.508
like prior to your father
passing away?

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.509
Had you always thought
you were going

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.011
to start something
and go build something?

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:26.312
Or do you think that
what ended up being

00:07:26.312 --> 00:07:27.513
a spark of wanting
to go create

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:28.948
something with your name
attached to it?

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:29.682
Yeah, it's funny.

00:07:29.682 --> 00:07:31.918
Like now I couldn't envision
doing anything

00:07:32.084 --> 00:07:33.953
but this.

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:36.088
But now growing up,

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.323
all I could
think about doing

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:39.325
was being a trader
on Wall Street

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:42.094
and an

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:43.830
strange and the reason

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.765
was, again,
that's what my parents did.

00:07:45.765 --> 00:07:48.334
So it wasn't like me
thinking on behalf of myself

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:50.536
or what I knew I enjoyed
versus didn't enjoy.

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:52.338
It was just like
my dad's a trader.

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:52.972
That's what he does.

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:55.041
My mom mom's a salesperson
at a bank.

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:56.142
That's what she does.

00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:57.510
I just want to be like them.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:58.611
And so I actually thought

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:00.513
it was the opposite
of independent thing.

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:03.015
He was dependent
thinking for my whole life.

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:05.251
I was for sure,
creative, growing up.

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:07.420
Like I remember as early
as like

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:08.821
first or second grade.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.022
This is like
the smallest thing.

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:11.657
But I remember

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:13.759
wanting to create

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.496
a pen highlighter combo.

00:08:16.496 --> 00:08:17.864
They didn't sell those
at the time,

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:20.166
so I snipped a pen in half
and I snipped

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:20.967
a highlighter in half.

00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:22.301
I taped them in the middle

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:23.903
and so I could both

00:08:23.903 --> 00:08:24.637
write notes

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:27.039
and highlight
with the same instrument.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:28.708
And then at sleepaway camp,

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:31.277
I had a shoe
