that's better.

00:16:35.794 --> 00:16:37.229
The true story probably

00:16:37.229 --> 00:16:38.597
live somewhere between.

00:16:38.597 --> 00:16:40.232
I've been writing this
for a little while,

00:16:40.232 --> 00:16:42.901
and the original product
was a microsoft Word

00:16:42.901 --> 00:16:44.603
template converted to a PDF

00:16:44.603 --> 00:16:46.105
attached to an email.

00:16:46.105 --> 00:16:47.940
There was no landing
page or website.

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:51.543
If you want a daily,
I would say was daily

00:16:51.543 --> 00:16:52.945
other than days
where I missed it.

00:16:52.945 --> 00:16:53.512
Okay.

00:16:53.712 --> 00:16:54.913
And how many people

00:16:54.913 --> 00:16:56.181
were on the list
in those early days?

00:16:56.181 --> 00:16:56.281
Yeah.

00:16:56.281 --> 00:16:58.884
So the first email
went out to like 45

00:16:58.884 --> 00:17:00.085
or 50 people

00:17:00.085 --> 00:17:01.487
and it started with

00:17:01.487 --> 00:17:03.422
like API people, people

00:17:03.422 --> 00:17:05.057
in the business school
and my family.

00:17:05.057 --> 00:17:06.091
And then I would just start

00:17:06.091 --> 00:17:07.626
getting messages
from people saying, Hey,

00:17:07.626 --> 00:17:09.061
I heard about your daily
roundup.

00:17:09.061 --> 00:17:10.662
Can you add media listserv

00:17:10.662 --> 00:17:12.064
and type in email addresses?

00:17:12.064 --> 00:17:14.099
It was just a listserv
I was managing.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:15.167
I got to

00:17:15.167 --> 00:17:17.536
I want to say
it was December

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:20.606
of 2014 or January of 2015,
and I sent out an email

00:17:20.639 --> 00:17:21.907
basically saying,

00:17:21.907 --> 00:17:23.642
I want
to take this more seriously.

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:25.744
I like

00:17:25.744 --> 00:17:26.912
I didn't
even call it a business,

00:17:26.912 --> 00:17:27.813
but I just want to take this

00:17:27.813 --> 00:17:29.281
to the next level
because there's appetite.

00:17:29.281 --> 00:17:30.482
Let me know
if you want to help out.

00:17:30.482 --> 00:17:32.618
And I sent that email
to my readers.

00:17:32.918 --> 00:17:34.787
Austin
was one of those readers.

00:17:34.787 --> 00:17:36.588
He reached out
saying he had ideas

00:17:36.588 --> 00:17:37.756
and he wanted to help.

00:17:37.756 --> 00:17:39.792
And we met in the Winter
Garden, which is like

00:17:39.792 --> 00:17:41.827
the main lobby area
of the business school,

00:17:42.027 --> 00:17:43.295
and we basically just talked

00:17:43.295 --> 00:17:45.731
for an hour about

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:47.666
what the future of this
newsletter could look like.

00:17:47.666 --> 00:17:49.201
That was like
our first conversation.

00:17:49.201 --> 00:17:51.703
So what piqued your interest
about it?

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:52.538
Austin Like what?

00:17:52.538 --> 00:17:53.405
Why did you get excited?

00:17:53.405 --> 00:17:55.074
Because you were,
if I recall correctly,

00:17:55.074 --> 00:17:56.642
you were younger than
at the time,

00:17:56.642 --> 00:17:58.844
but you were on a similar
finance

00:17:59.011 --> 00:18:00.212
looking path.

00:18:00.212 --> 00:18:02.381
Yeah, So I was a sophomore

00:18:02.381 --> 00:18:04.416
and I say
there were two reasons.

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:06.718
One was high level
and one was about the

00:18:06.952 --> 00:18:08.020
so high level.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:09.755
By sophomore year

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:11.457
I had started to ask them
questions

00:18:11.457 --> 00:18:13.892
about about this investment
banking path,

00:18:13.892 --> 00:18:15.661
and I was like, Hmm, I'm

00:18:15.661 --> 00:18:17.863
not sure if this is actually
what I want to do.

00:18:17.863 --> 00:18:19.698
I talked to people,
had done this banking thing,

00:18:19.698 --> 00:18:20.899
and it seemed kind of

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:22.234
terrible, Like it seemed

00:18:22.234 --> 00:18:23.769
pretty awful,

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.604
you know, a hundred
hour weeks and building

00:18:25.604 --> 00:18:26.405
Excel models

00:18:26.405 --> 00:18:27.973
and 8 to 10 years before

00:18:27.973 --> 00:18:29.908
you have
any real responsibility.

00:18:29.908 --> 00:18:31.343
And I said,
I kind of want to hedge

00:18:31.343 --> 00:18:33.078
in case
this thing doesn't work.

00:18:33.078 --> 00:18:34.513
I had a couple friends
who were more

00:18:34.513 --> 00:18:36.381
entrepreneurial minded.

00:18:36.381 --> 00:18:37.683
I started

00:18:37.683 --> 00:18:38.350
I actually listen

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:39.551
to a podcast series

00:18:39.551 --> 00:18:40.853
that I'm sure

00:18:40.853 --> 00:18:42.354
I assume you've listened to.

00:18:42.354 --> 00:18:44.156
It was a Sam Altman class

00:18:44.156 --> 00:18:45.424
at Stanford.

00:18:45.424 --> 00:18:46.525
It's ten episodes.

00:18:46.525 --> 00:18:48.494
It's you know, Paul
Graham does one, but

00:18:48.494 --> 00:18:49.828
I had to start a startup.

00:18:49.828 --> 00:18:51.263
Yeah, I think it's called
How to Start a Startup.

00:18:51.263 --> 00:18:52.731
And I listened to that.

00:18:52.731 --> 00:18:54.366
I was like, Whoa,
this is way

00:18:54.366 --> 00:18:56.168
cooler than the whole
banking thing.

00:18:56.168 --> 00:18:57.136
Like how do I get into this?

00:18:57.136 --> 00:18:58.737
So I always had
this idea that

00:18:58.737 --> 00:18:59.605
let me hedge

00:18:59.605 --> 00:19:01.373
in case the banking thing
isn't for me.

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:04.510
Still, 199% thought
I was going to banking.

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.279
I spent the summer after
sophomore year banking,

00:19:07.279 --> 00:19:08.013
and then selfishly,

00:19:08.013 --> 00:19:09.548
I read the morning
brew thing

00:19:09.548 --> 00:19:10.983
and or the market corner
thing

00:19:10.983 --> 00:19:12.384
at the time and I was like,

00:19:13.819 --> 00:19:14.987
This is
actually pretty good.

00:19:14.987 --> 00:19:16.622
No one reads the Wall Street
Journal.

00:19:16.622 --> 00:19:18.390
You walk into the business
school, you'd walk down

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.459
the stairs, there's
a stack of ten newspapers

00:19:20.459 --> 00:19:21.660
and you leave at night

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:22.928
and that staff would
still be there.

00:19:22.928 --> 00:19:24.463
Basically don't read it.

00:19:24.463 --> 00:19:26.398
And I bought into the idea

00:19:26.398 --> 00:19:28.500
that you could actually make

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.035
the business world
more engaging. Now.

00:19:30.035 --> 00:19:31.436
I think it was
a real business.

00:19:31.436 --> 00:19:33.605
No, look, I agreed
with that, professor,

00:19:34.173 --> 00:19:35.641
but there was I don't know,

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:36.909
there was something there
about it.

00:19:36.909 --> 00:19:39.044
And also, to be honest, like

00:19:39.044 --> 00:19:40.445
people were signing up

00:19:40.445 --> 00:19:41.813
and I was reading,
but it wasn't good.

00:19:41.813 --> 00:19:43.315
Like the no PR answers.

00:19:43.315 --> 00:19:44.550
It looked horrible.

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:45.450
Like it was terrible.

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.186
I don't think a picture
that we can put up,

00:19:47.186 --> 00:19:48.420
but the original newsletter

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.055
looked terrible.

00:19:50.055 --> 00:19:52.457
There was a bull in a bear

00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:53.892
like the fighting word

00:19:53.892 --> 00:19:55.727
or fighting
like it looked horrible.

00:19:55.727 --> 00:19:56.261
Yeah, I see.

00:19:56.261 --> 00:19:57.496
If I'm reading
this thing, it's

00:19:57.496 --> 00:19:58.897
written like, okay.

00:19:58.897 --> 00:20:00.732
And it
and it looks like shit.

00:20:00.732 --> 00:20:02.100
It's written okay.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:02.834
I mean, it was.

00:20:02.834 --> 00:20:04.102
Yeah, he was reading the

00:20:04.102 --> 00:20:05.504
entire Wall Street
Journal cover

00:20:05.504 --> 00:20:07.372
to cover and summarizing it
like 3 hours.

00:20:07.372 --> 00:20:08.707
How could it have been?

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:10.809
But like, but
I still read it every day.

00:20:10.809 --> 00:20:12.611
And so I think it was that,
it was the classic thing

00:20:12.611 --> 00:20:13.979
where product sucks,

00:20:13.979 --> 00:20:15.647
but people still use it

00:20:15.714 --> 00:20:17.049
like, oh,
I mean, it's like Amazon.

00:20:17.049 --> 00:20:18.784
Amazon look terrible, right?

00:20:18.784 --> 00:20:20.219
It's a worse
design site that

00:20:20.219 --> 00:20:21.820
I use, yet
people, people use it.

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:22.654
So I thought
that was interesting.

00:20:22.654 --> 00:20:24.056
The thing
that's so interesting to me

00:20:24.056 --> 00:20:25.257
when I hear the story

00:20:25.257 --> 00:20:27.192
recounted is like,

00:20:27.192 --> 00:20:27.993
I mean, we're

00:20:27.993 --> 00:20:30.229
doing a podcast called
The Crazy Ones.

00:20:30.229 --> 00:20:30.729
This is like

00:20:30.729 --> 00:20:32.030
kind of a crazy idea

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:33.465
at the time
from a business standpoint,

00:20:33.465 --> 00:20:35.434
like today,
it's super obvious

00:20:35.434 --> 00:20:36.969
to say, like
you can start a newsletter,

00:20:36.969 --> 00:20:38.770
you can monetize it
via sponsors,

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.272
you can like build
a business,

00:20:40.272 --> 00:20:41.840
you can do courses, product,
like there's

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.141
so much opportunity around

00:20:43.141 --> 00:20:44.643
an email list at the time

00:20:44.643 --> 00:20:47.279
that wasn't really a thing
like the creator economy.

00:20:47.279 --> 00:20:48.180
There was no V.C.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:50.249
that was like, Yeah, I'm
a creator economy investor.

00:20:50.249 --> 00:20:51.483
You know,
that wasn't a thing

00:20:51.483 --> 00:20:52.751
that people
were going after.

00:20:52.751 --> 00:20:54.152
And yet you guys had this

00:20:54.152 --> 00:20:55.787
spark and decided

00:20:55.787 --> 00:20:57.689
to start doing it
and pursuing it.

00:20:57.689 --> 00:20:59.024
Part of
it sounds like it was like

00:20:59.024 --> 00:21:00.692
you had time on your hands

00:21:00.692 --> 00:21:01.126
and part of

00:21:01.126 --> 00:21:03.528
it sounds like your
alternative kind of sucked

00:21:03.528 --> 00:21:04.896
and you weren't
that excited about it.

00:21:04.896 --> 00:21:06.365
Yeah, but

00:21:06.365 --> 00:21:07.366
I think there's just,

00:21:07.366 --> 00:21:08.867
just a few things that

00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:10.168
that made it successful

00:21:10.168 --> 00:21:11.670
in the early days.

00:21:11.737 --> 00:21:14.973
One, we didn't intend for it
to be a business,

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:16.675
and if we did

00:21:16.675 --> 00:21:18.977
and we thought about it
as a business in 2015,

00:21:18.977 --> 00:21:19.978
I think it would have
failed.

00:21:19.978 --> 00:21:20.979
We would have made long,

00:21:20.979 --> 00:21:22.781
short term decisions
to make a little money

00:21:22.781 --> 00:21:24.082
here or there,
but because of

00:21:24.082 --> 00:21:25.484
the side project, we didn't.

00:21:25.484 --> 00:21:27.152
That's an incredibly
important insight,

00:21:27.152 --> 00:21:28.954
by the way,
for all listeners.

00:21:28.954 --> 00:21:30.989
I mean, I talk about this
constantly of like

00:21:31.156 --> 00:21:32.457
whenever anyone

00:21:32.457 --> 00:21:33.692
started something,

00:21:33.692 --> 00:21:34.926
they were just pursuing it

00:21:34.926 --> 00:21:37.029
out of like pure passion,

00:21:37.029 --> 00:21:38.330
interest, excitement

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:39.898
and joy of actually
doing it.

00:21:39.898 --> 00:21:41.133
The process on a daily

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:42.601
basis was so amazing to them

00:21:42.601 --> 00:21:44.369
and that paradoxically,

00:21:44.369 --> 00:21:46.371
is actually what allowed it
to scale and succeed.

00:21:46.371 --> 00:21:46.738
They weren't

00:21:46.738 --> 00:21:49.174
doing it for the like,
Hey, let me monetize.

00:21:49.174 --> 00:21:50.776
When I get to 5000
customers, I'm

00:21:50.776 --> 00:21:53.045
going to build my six
figure revenue stream.

00:21:53.045 --> 00:21:54.980
Like those things
are really, really difficult

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:56.548
because then you're
just focused on price

00:21:56.548 --> 00:21:57.382
the whole way along the way,

00:21:57.382 --> 00:21:58.650
you're not process oriented.

00:21:58.650 --> 00:21:59.051
So that's like,

00:21:59.051 --> 00:22:00.185
I think that's
a super important

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:01.620
insight for people
to take away Totally.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.155
And we got there at 2017.

00:22:03.155 --> 00:22:05.424
We always kind of sold
the math and

00:22:05.424 --> 00:22:06.391
I mean so many people
saying, Hey,

00:22:06.391 --> 00:22:07.826
this newsletter thing is
dumb.

00:22:07.826 --> 00:22:08.894
And Alex
and I would sit down

00:22:08.894 --> 00:22:10.395
like once a week
or once a month and we'd

00:22:10.395 --> 00:22:12.364
look at the Excel model
and be like,

00:22:12.364 --> 00:22:14.733
These people are telling us
we're stupid,

00:22:14.733 --> 00:22:16.068
but we were acquiring

00:22:16.068 --> 00:22:17.736
subscribers for X,

00:22:17.736 --> 00:22:19.638
we're making Y off them.

00:22:19.638 --> 00:22:20.639
So if we can get the list

00:22:20.639 --> 00:22:23.041
from 50000 to 100000,

00:22:23.041 --> 00:22:25.110
I think the profit numbers
are going to double.

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:26.812
And then I think
we get to 200,000

00:22:26.812 --> 00:22:28.347
and I think there were
a couple of moments,

00:22:28.347 --> 00:22:29.214
I dunno
if you felt this way,

00:22:29.214 --> 00:22:30.382
but there were a couple
moments where I was like,

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:32.484
either

00:22:32.684 --> 00:22:34.653
we have
the numbers, so either

00:22:34.653 --> 00:22:35.854
no one else sees this

00:22:35.854 --> 00:22:37.689
or we're complete idiots.

00:22:37.689 --> 00:22:39.458
And, and I think

00:22:39.458 --> 00:22:39.958
there were times

00:22:39.958 --> 00:22:40.959
where we second
guessed ourselves

00:22:40.959 --> 00:22:42.527
because we're like
the numbers are so clear.

00:22:42.527 --> 00:22:44.463
It's the most obvious
Excel model in the world.

00:22:44.463 --> 00:22:45.731
Cost stay the same,

00:22:45.731 --> 00:22:46.898
revenues go up,

00:22:46.898 --> 00:22:48.467
profit goes up.

00:22:48.633 --> 00:22:49.034
Yeah.

00:22:49.034 --> 00:22:50.435
I mean, I just think

00:22:50.435 --> 00:22:51.403
the best part about it

00:22:51.403 --> 00:22:53.171
is how simple of a business
it is.

00:22:53.171 --> 00:22:55.974
Yet no one understood
how simple it was.

00:22:56.308 --> 00:22:57.242
There's a few things

00:22:57.242 --> 00:22:59.111
that I would add, which is

00:22:59.111 --> 00:23:00.545
I think
the point about something

00:23:00.545 --> 00:23:01.980
not feeling like a business
from day

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:04.516
one is such
an important thing

00:23:04.516 --> 00:23:06.818
because it reminds me of

00:23:07.753 --> 00:23:10.222
when I, I interviewed
Tim Ferriss.

00:23:10.222 --> 00:23:12.524
And one thing he had
said was like,

00:23:12.524 --> 00:23:15.494
he loves interviewing
non business people,

00:23:15.494 --> 00:23:17.162
like people
who are just like

00:23:17.162 --> 00:23:18.363
on the fringes

00:23:18.363 --> 00:23:20.899
of the Internet and society
who are like,

00:23:20.899 --> 00:23:22.801
when I talked to him, he
I said, What are you going

00:23:22.801 --> 00:23:23.969
down the rabbit
hole of right now?

00:23:23.969 --> 00:23:25.404
And he was like,

00:23:25.404 --> 00:23:27.672
archery and compound bows,

00:23:28.907 --> 00:23:30.175
animal tracking

00:23:30.175 --> 00:23:31.276
and one other thing.

00:23:31.276 --> 00:23:32.844
And but he gave me
the reason.

00:23:32.844 --> 00:23:33.311
He's like,

00:23:33.311 --> 00:23:34.379
because the people

00:23:34.379 --> 00:23:35.747
who are best

00:23:35.747 --> 00:23:37.382
point 1% in the world
at this

00:23:37.382 --> 00:23:40.085
are so genuinely obsessed
with the craft

00:23:40.218 --> 00:23:41.653
because they can't be in it
for the money

00:23:41.653 --> 00:23:42.921
because it doesn't pay well.

00:23:42.921 --> 00:23:43.922
And there's something so

00:23:43.922 --> 00:23:45.957
like amazing about
that to me

00:23:45.957 --> 00:23:47.192
is like you actually

00:23:47.192 --> 00:23:49.761
go to people who can't
be motivated by money

00:23:49.761 --> 00:23:50.996
and you find
just like kind of

00:23:50.996 --> 00:23:53.098
this unbridled

00:23:53.098 --> 00:23:54.499
passion for the thing

00:23:54.499 --> 00:23:55.801
and for the journey of it,

00:23:55.801 --> 00:23:56.701
I think that's huge.

00:23:56.701 --> 00:23:58.003
I think, you know,

00:23:58.003 --> 00:24:00.105
the other thing is

00:24:00.105 --> 00:24:02.240
you talk about this
a fair bit

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:04.676
like you've talked about on
Twitter, is like increasing

00:24:04.676 --> 00:24:06.077
your luck surface area.

00:24:06.077 --> 00:24:08.113
And I think that was
absolutely a part of it.

00:24:08.113 --> 00:24:10.415
Like there were things
we did in the early days

00:24:10.415 --> 00:24:12.784
to increase our luck surface
area, like

00:24:12.784 --> 00:24:13.652
making it

00:24:13.652 --> 00:24:15.754
like making the decision
to write this thing.

00:24:15.754 --> 00:24:17.155
Like if I didn't make
the decision

00:24:17.155 --> 00:24:18.457
to write this thing,
I could have very easily

00:24:18.457 --> 00:24:20.959
just played FIFA and NHL or
senior year, but I didn't.

00:24:20.959 --> 00:24:21.893
I decide
to write this thing.

00:24:21.893 --> 00:24:23.195
So that increase
of the surface area,

00:24:23.195 --> 00:24:25.564
a little reaching out
to those professors

00:24:25.564 --> 00:24:26.932
or possible investors

00:24:26.932 --> 00:24:28.033
and doing it relentlessly,

00:24:28.033 --> 00:24:29.835
irrespective of
if they gave us a

00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:31.970
yes or no increased
luck surface area.

00:24:32.304 --> 00:24:33.538
But there's also
another piece

00:24:33.538 --> 00:24:35.841
where, like I think
there truly was luck.

